<!-- ============================== ENTER MY WORLD (carried over from GhostHeart_Story.html + woman-behind-the-scenes) ============================== -->
<div class="view" data-view="story" hidden>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="pagehead proj-head">
        <p class="mono" style="margin:0 0 6px">One lived story / many voices</p>
        <h1><span class="chrome">Enter</span> <span class="red">My World.</span></h1>
        <p>GhostHeart is the fictional mirror of a real life shaped by survival, identity, relationships, loss, and change.</p>
        <p>The music gives those experiences a voice. Here, there is room for the life behind the name.</p>
        <div class="row" style="margin-top:10px"><a class="btn ghost" href="#music">Hear the music</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#connect">Get email updates</a></div>
      </div>
      <article class="read panel">
        <p class="mono k">01 · The life behind the name</p>
        <h2>Behind the Scars — Before the Heart Wakes</h2>
        <p class="sub">Not one wound. Not one label. Still here.</p>
          <p>I kept seeing people treated like they were nothing.</p>
          <p>People without a home being picked on. People struggling with addiction being laughed at. LGBTQ people getting torn apart for existing. Mental illness turned into a joke. Everybody pointing a finger while somebody else stood there taking the damage.</p>
          <p>And I had plenty to say about it.</p>
          <p>If I’d been there, I would’ve said something. I would’ve stood up for them. I’d find time to help at a shelter. Make a stand. Do something that mattered.</p>
          <p>I kept saying it.</p>
          <p>I got angry at the news, the schools, the government. Why the hell was this happening? Why was it allowed? People were supposed to be protected. Every one of them.</p>
          <p>But I wasn’t doing shit about it.</p>
          <p>That was hard to admit, because I really did care. I cared enough to get angry. Enough to hurt over it. But stepping forward meant giving people a reason to look at me.</p>
          <p>My faults. My past. Everything they could pick apart.</p>
          <p>There’s a lot in my life somebody could judge. There are also choices I have to own. What happened to me doesn’t erase my responsibility for what I’ve done. Telling my story means being honest about that, too.</p>
          <p>I was afraid of being ridiculed. Afraid of starting fights. Afraid that trying to help somebody else would give the world another chance to tear me down.</p>
          <p>So I sat there, caring, hurting, and complaining.</p>
          <p>Eventually, I had to face how little that was changing.</p>
          <p>My first idea was a website where people could write how they felt. Looking back, I thought it was dumb. I spent a year struggling to build apps and figure out social media, failing at things I wanted so badly to get right.</p>
          <p>Then I finished Axiomara. And I came up with GhostHeart.</p>
          <p>A way to hide and still tell my story.</p>
          <p>GhostHeart carries the bravery, the strength, the courage I’m still trying to find in myself. He gives me a way to step forward while part of me still wants to stay hidden.</p>
          <p>I’m the man behind him. The one who cares deeply and is still scared.</p>
          <p>That’s where the awakening began: admitting that I could spend my life being angry about people’s pain without ever doing anything useful with my own.</p>
          <p>Maybe my story could help somebody. Even with the ugly parts. Even with the mistakes I have to own. Even if the world judged me for telling it.</p>
          <p>I don’t know how many people will read these words. I don’t know if I’ll ever stop being afraid of what comes next.</p>
          <p>But if I help one person, then I did something.</p>
          <p>I finally did more than sit there and wish somebody would.</p>
        <div class="row"><a class="btn red" href="#films">Watch This Is GhostHeart</a><a class="btn ghost" href="#films">Watch GhostHeart’s Oath</a></div>
      </article>
      <article class="read panel">
        <p class="mono k">02 · The relationship behind the work</p>
        <h2>The Woman Behind the Scenes.</h2>
        <p class="sub">Her love helped shape the man he is today. Meet GhostHeart’s Rebel.</p>
        <details class="more"><summary>Read The Woman Behind the Scenes</summary>
          <p>I was confused about love.</p>
          <p>Some people held hands and made it look easy. Others said they screamed because they cared. Some of what people called love left bruises. Some love left.</p>
          <p>I didn’t understand how all of that could live under the same word.</p>
          <p>But I knew what I wanted.</p>
          <p>I wanted my grandparents’ love.</p>
          <p>Married within six months. Kids. A house. Fifty-four years together. From where I stood, they always worked as a team. I didn’t see yelling or fighting. I saw two people who made loving each other look like the easiest thing in the world.</p>
          <p>They were perfect to me.</p>
          <p>I wanted that. I just didn’t understand that I could spend my life looking for their love and miss the love standing right in front of me.</p>
          <p>Because I’m not my grandpa. And my life didn’t look like his.</p>
          <p>I knew my momma loved me. I knew I could give somebody my whole heart. But giving everything I had didn’t mean I knew how to love well. I was still learning how to be the man I wanted to be. How to be a good husband. How to recognize love when it asked something difficult of me.</p>
          <p>I hadn’t understood that love could fight for you. That sometimes it meant helping you stand, and sometimes it meant challenging what you were standing for.</p>
          <p>Then my wife took my hand, and it felt like a whole new universe.</p>
          <p>In GhostHeart’s world, you’ll know her as GhostHeart’s Rebel.</p>
          <p>There’s a whole story in how we found each other. We’ll get there. This one begins with the challenge Rebel put in front of me.</p>
          <p>I wanted to help people like me. Build a place for the broken ones. The people whose pain I recognized because I’d carried some of it myself.</p>
          <p>I thought that was where change began.</p>
          <p>Rebel made me look at who I was leaving outside.</p>
          <p>If I wanted the world to stop separating people, why was I building another line between us? What about the people whose lives I didn’t understand? The ones who didn’t look like me, love like me, or carry the same scars?</p>
          <p>Did they deserve less kindness because their pain wasn’t familiar?</p>
          <p>That got to me.</p>
          <p>I had been telling her how much I cared. How angry I was. How badly I wanted the world to change. Rebel challenged me to make that care bigger than the people I already knew how to stand beside.</p>
          <p>Everyone needed love. Everyone needed room to be human. If I wanted to bring people together, I had to mean it when somebody different walked through the door.</p>
          <p>And I had to do more than tell her about it.</p>
          <p>I had to tell the world. More than that, I had to start showing it.</p>
          <p>She got to me.</p>
          <p>I wanted to make a change. Rebel made me face where that change had to begin.</p>
        </details>
        <p class="pull">She saw the heart beneath the scars — and never asked it to hide.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="read-end">
        <p class="mono" style="margin:0 0 4px">03 · Stay with what speaks to you</p>
        <h2 class="giant"><span class="chrome">Your pace.</span> <span class="red">Your way in.</span></h2>
        <p style="color:var(--silver);max-width:62ch">You do not have to share this past to find something in these words. Stay with a song. Carry a line that means something to you. Find support when you need more than a story can offer.</p>
        <ul class="help-kinds three">
          <li class="panel"><a href="#music"><span class="mono">01</span><b>Stay with a Song</b>The music</a></li>
          <li class="panel"><a href="#home"><span class="mono">02</span><b>Carry a Line</b>Today’s line</a></li>
          <li class="panel"><a href="#support"><span class="mono">03</span><b>Find Support</b>Help &amp; resources</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="note">No one has to disclose their own pain to belong here.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<!-- ============================== PRIVACY (carried over from GhostHeart_Privacy.html, same words) ============================== -->
<div class="view" data-view="privacy" hidden>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap legal">
      <p class="mono" style="margin:0 0 6px">Privacy</p>
      <h1 class="giant"><span class="chrome">Your inbox</span> <span class="red">stays yours.</span></h1>
      <p>This notice covers website visit analytics, blog comments, the email address you choose to submit through the Stay Connected form, and the optional third-party film players on this website.</p>
      <h2>Website visit analytics</h2>
      <p>GhostHeart uses Metricool to measure visits and pages viewed on this website. Its tracking script connects to Metricool when a page loads. Metricool states that this tracker uses no cookies or unique visitor identifiers, does not store visitors' IP addresses, and provides anonymous, aggregate statistics. An IP address is processed during the connection to determine the visitor's country. These statistics help GhostHeart understand how the website is used. Learn more about <a href="https://help.metricool.com/use-of-cookies-in-metricool-v5dg7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Metricool's website tracker</a> and its <a href="https://metricool.com/privacy-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy practices</a>.</p>
      <h2>Blog comments</h2>
      <p>If you submit a comment on a blog entry, GhostHeart stores your chosen display name, comment, entry reference, submission time, and moderation status in its Supabase project. No login or email address is required. A moderator reads each submission before it appears. If approved, your display name, comment, and approval date appear publicly. Please do not include private or crisis details.</p>
      <p>To limit abuse, the comment service processes the connection address into a keyed marker. The comment tables store that marker and attempt time, not the raw address. The hosting provider may separately process connection data in its service logs. Abuse markers are deleted after two days. Pending and rejected comments are deleted after 30 days. Moderation decision records are deleted after 90 days. Approved comments remain until removed.</p>
      <p>To request removal of an approved comment, email <a href="mailto:signal@myghostheart.com">signal@myghostheart.com</a> and identify the blog entry and comment. You may read the blog without submitting a comment.</p>
      <h2>Films and YouTube</h2>
      <p>Most GhostHeart film thumbnails load from this website. Two older Shorts in the film catalog may request a thumbnail from YouTube when you view that catalog. Embedded YouTube players load only when you press a film frame. When you do, the privacy-enhanced YouTube player may receive connection and playback information under YouTube and Google's own privacy practices. You can browse the rest of the website without loading a YouTube player.</p>
      <h2>Saved on this device</h2>
      <p>If you save a song, film or line, or stop partway through a song or a reading, this website remembers it in your own browser (local storage) so you can check back in later. It also keeps a visit count and the time of your last visit, to welcome you back and show what is new. None of this is sent to GhostHeart or anyone else. Clear it any time by clearing this site’s data in your browser.</p>
      <h2>What is collected</h2>
      <p>The signup form asks for your email address. GhostHeart does not ask for a password, payment information, or sensitive personal information through this form.</p>
      <h2>Why it is collected</h2>
      <p>Your email address is used to send GhostHeart updates such as new songs, films, stories, resources, and project news. You must confirm the subscription before messages are sent.</p>
      <h2>Who processes it</h2>
      <p>The form is processed by Mailchimp. When you submit it, Mailchimp receives the information and handles the confirmation and mailing-list record under its own privacy practices.</p>
      <h2>Your control</h2>
      <ul><li>You can leave the form blank and continue using the rest of the website.</li><li>You can ignore the confirmation message and remain unsubscribed.</li><li>You can unsubscribe from any GhostHeart Heartbeat email using the link included in that message.</li></ul>
      <h2>No sale of signup information</h2>
      <p>GhostHeart does not sell the email address you provide through this signup form.</p>
      <p><a href="#connect">Return to Stay Connected</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<!-- ============================== BLOG: carried over + new idea ============================== -->
<div class="view" data-view="blog" hidden>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="alt-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Show version" data-for="blog"><button type="button" class="on" data-alt="a">Carried over</button><button type="button" data-alt="b">New idea</button></div>
      <div class="alt alt-a">
        <div class="pagehead proj-head">
          <p class="mono" style="margin:0 0 6px">Behind the songs · GhostHeart / Blog</p>
          <h1><span class="chrome">The GhostHeart</span> <span class="red">Blog.</span></h1>
          <p>A short note with every new song and film. Choose an entry below by title and date. The full life story lives on <a href="https://toohumantostayquiet.substack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Substack ↗</a>.</p>
          <p>The songs don’t tell you everything. Here I write about the thoughts behind them and the questions they leave with me.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="mono" style="margin:16px 0 6px">Latest entry first</p>
        <div class="entries">
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-papa"><summary><span class="mono k">Passage 04 · October 31, 2026</span><b>Papa Didn’t Raise No Fool — A Backbone and a Heart</b><span class="lead">The lesson is to keep thinking for yourself without losing your heart.</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>Content note: This reading reflects on family loss, fear, judgment and love, without graphic detail.</p>
<p>Some lessons stay with us because we keep finding places to use them.</p>
<p>In Papa Didn’t Raise No Fool, the narrator remembers hours on a front porch, listening to Papa’s stories. There is affection in that picture, but the stories carry difficult things too: fear, loss and a world that can be harder to understand than it first appears.</p>
<p>Papa takes the questions seriously. He can be stern. He can also send the narrator back to the books with a joke about needing a doctor. The song gives us someone whose care has more than one voice.</p>
<p>At the center of what he teaches are two things that belong together.</p>
<p>Papa didn’t raise no fool He raised me with a backbone and a heart</p>
<p>A backbone gives a person something to stand on when it would be easier to agree for the sake of being accepted. A heart keeps that strength connected to the people affected by what we say and do.</p>
<p>Holding both can take work. We can become so concerned with proving we cannot be fooled that we stop being willing to learn. Or we can become so eager to keep everyone comfortable that we leave our own judgment unused.</p>
<p>Papa’s lesson asks for attention.</p>
<p>Look closely at what is being offered. Take time with the words. A confident voice, an impressive appearance or a familiar place on a screen cannot do our thinking for us. We still have to consider what a claim rests on and remain willing to ask another question.</p>
<p>There is humility in that responsibility. Thinking for yourself includes recognizing when you do not know enough. It includes learning something that changes your mind. Being difficult to fool does not require pretending you have every answer.</p>
<p>The song makes that especially clear when it returns to learning.</p>
<p>The smartest man I ever knew Never finished school</p>
<p>That is the narrator’s regard for one person, not a reason to give up on education. Papa points toward the books himself. The lesson makes room for knowledge gained through study and for wisdom carried in an ordinary life. A person’s title cannot tell us everything worth hearing from them.</p>
<p>The same care belongs in how we judge people.</p>
<p>When Papa talks about love, he refuses to let other people’s shame decide its value. His advice leaves room for a heart to be honest. The strength he is teaching has to include that kindness; otherwise, the backbone has lost something essential.</p>
<p>The final chorus brings fear and hate into the lesson too. They can press for a quick answer about who to follow, whom to distrust and what another person deserves. The song asks the narrator to keep hold of a self that those pressures do not get to command.</p>
<p>That choice reaches beyond a declaration. It can show up in checking a claim before passing it on, hearing someone out before assigning them a motive, or standing by a value when the room would prefer easy agreement.</p>
<p>It can also show up in admitting a mistake and taking the trouble to correct it.</p>
<p>The narrator carries Papa’s words forward because they still have somewhere to go. The porch is the beginning of the lesson. Life beyond it is where the lesson has to be practiced.</p>
<p>He didn’t need a fancy title Didn’t need no polished name</p>
<p>What matters is what the teaching asks of the person who receives it: keep learning, look carefully, love honestly and stand for something without letting cruelty become part of the stance.</p>
<p>A backbone and a heart. Both need to come with us into the next conversation, the next difficult choice, and the next chance to decide what kind of person we want to be.</p>
<p class="note">Some parts of the story are hard to hear. You can pause or step away. If you need support now, see Find Support. <a href="#support">Find Support</a></p></div></details>
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-wth-week"><summary><span class="mono k">Announcement · October 30, 2026</span><b>Wake the Heart Week</b><span class="lead">November 9 to 15: one week inside Before the Heart Wakes, built around the songs and the people they are for.</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>From Monday, November 9 to Sunday, November 15, Before the Heart Wakes gets a week of its own: Wake the Heart Week.</p>
<p>Before the Heart Wakes is eight songs that follow one journey, from witnessing cruelty to finding the courage to stand beside someone else. For one week, every place GhostHeart shows up will carry a different piece of that same story. One heart. Never the same post twice.</p>
<p>Here is what is already set. On Saturday, November 14 at 7 PM Central, Passage 06 opens: the reading for This Is GhostHeart. On Sunday, November 15 at 10 AM Central, the week closes with the Sunday Live.</p>
<p>A Friday night listening event and the first letter to the email list are planned for the same week. Their details will be posted here and sent by email once they are confirmed.</p>
<p>You don’t have to take part in everything. Stay with one song. Carry one line. If someone comes to mind while you listen, that might be the person to share it with.</p>
<p>What survived can help someone rise.</p></div></details>
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-fifties"><summary><span class="mono k">Passage 03 · October 30, 2026</span><b>It’s Not the 1950s</b><span class="lead">Change came from the people who refused to wait for it.</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>Some lessons arrive as stories told across a kitchen table.</p>
<p>It’s Not the 1950s grows out of one of those. Grandma told it the way people tell things they have watched happen more than once: the government makes a law, the people push back, and years later the government takes the credit for the change the people fought for.</p>
<p>She was not talking about one fight. She was talking about a pattern.</p>
<p>Slavery did not end because the people in power woke up one day and decided it was wrong. People resisted, escaped, organized and refused to stop. Women did not get the vote because it was handed to them. They marched and argued and kept showing up long after they were told to go home. Same-sex couples did not win the right to marry because someone at the top changed their mind first. They lived openly, told their stories and fought in court until the law caught up.</p>
<p>Each time, the history books can make it sound like the change came from above. Grandma wanted me to remember where it really started.</p>
<p>The change was won by people. The credit was taken by power.</p>
<p>That is not a reason to give up on laws. Laws matter. They can protect people or leave them exposed. It is a reason to remember who moves them.</p>
<p>And the story is not finished. Women today are still fighting for the right to make their own choices about their own lives. Saying “it’s not the 1950s” is not the same as saying the work is done. It is a reminder that the calendar moving forward does not guarantee that freedom moves with it.</p>
<p>What Grandma passed on was not anger for its own sake. It was attention. Notice who is doing the fighting. Notice who shows up to take the bow. And when it is your turn, do not wait for permission to stand up for someone’s freedom, including your own.</p>
<p>The people who changed things were ordinary people who refused to stay quiet.</p>
<p>That door is still open to anyone willing to walk through it.</p>
<p class="note">Some parts of the story are hard to hear. You can pause or step away. If you need support now, see Find Support. <a href="#support">Find Support</a></p></div></details>
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-im-not"><summary><span class="mono k">Passage 02 · October 19, 2026</span><b>I’m Not — More Than One Chapter</b><span class="lead">A story can be worth telling before the fear is gone.</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>Sometimes the hardest part of telling a story comes before the first word.</p>
<p>You may know what you want to say and still hear all the ways it could be misunderstood. Someone could choose one sentence, one mistake, one difficult chapter, and decide they have heard enough to explain your whole life.</p>
<p>That fear sits at the beginning of I’m Not. Before the narrator has finished speaking, judgment is already anticipated. The wish to be understood has to share the room with the fear of being reduced to a few words.</p>
<p>And still, there is something worth saying.</p>
<p>I just want to tell my story, So maybe someone will do the same.</p>
<p>There is room in that word “maybe.” It leaves the outcome open. A story might reach someone. It might help them find words for something they have carried quietly. It might give them a reason to consider a next step. The narrator cannot decide what another person will take from it.</p>
<p>What the narrator can choose is whether to speak honestly.</p>
<p>That does not require a perfect ending. There can still be mistakes, uncertainty and parts that are hard to explain. A person can be learning how to live with their choices and still have something worth sharing. If every story had to wait until its teller had nothing left to work on, a great many voices would never be heard.</p>
<p>The song makes room for a person who kept trying.</p>
<p>That matters when a single chapter has become the only one anyone wants to discuss. What went wrong can take up so much space that the effort afterward becomes difficult to see: continuing, trying again, choosing what to do next. Those parts belong to the story too.</p>
<p>No one else can fit a whole person into the worst thing they hear about them. Listening asks more of us than that. It asks us to leave room for what we have not heard, to notice when we are filling in the gaps ourselves, and to let someone finish before we decide what their words must mean.</p>
<p>For the person speaking, honesty can have boundaries.</p>
<p>I’m just opening up the door To the parts I used to hide.</p>
<p>An open door does not mean every room has to be shown. You can choose the part you are ready to put into words. You can keep names, details and other people’s lives private. You can stop where you need to stop. The value of what you share does not depend on giving an audience everything.</p>
<p>And listening to someone else’s story places no obligation on you to reveal your own.</p>
<p>You can recognize a feeling without having lived the same life. You can sit with a line, think about it privately, or leave something unspoken. There is no test of how much you must disclose before the song is allowed to mean something to you.</p>
<p>The bridge holds on to that freedom. Hearing that someone kept going cannot undo all the hurt. The song leaves that truth intact. Its hope is smaller and more possible: perhaps there is still a next step you can choose.</p>
<p>Maybe that step begins with finding words. Maybe it begins with giving yourself time. It belongs to you, and no one else can decide its shape from a distance.</p>
<p>I’m Not ends with an invitation that leaves room for someday.</p>
<p>A story can be offered without applause. It can remain imperfect and still be honest. Speaking can feel frightening and still be a choice worth considering.</p>
<p>For anyone listening, the responsibility is simple enough to begin now: hear the person, leave room for the chapters you do not know, and resist turning one part of a life into the whole of it.</p>
<p>For anyone considering their own words, there is time. You can choose what belongs in the telling and what stays yours.</p>
<p>The fear does not have to disappear before a voice can matter.</p>
<p class="note">Some parts of the story are hard to hear. You can pause or step away. If you need support now, see Find Support. <a href="#support">Find Support</a></p></div></details>
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-cruel"><summary><span class="mono k">Song reading · October 10, 2026</span><b>Cruel — Something Needs to Change</b><span class="lead">What happens after the video ends?</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>That’s the question I want Cruel to leave with us. The crowd moves on, but the person we watched still has to live with what happened.</p>
<p>It’s easy to see one reaction and think we understand someone. What might we be missing? What has made it hard for that person to trust kindness?</p>
<p>One conversation won’t undo years of hurt. Sometimes someone needs more help than we can offer. But a patient response can still matter: time to finish a sentence, a question asked without judgement, or kindness that doesn’t depend on hearing their whole story.</p>
<p>If you’re the person who is hurting, you don’t owe anyone your whole story to deserve kindness.</p>
<p>For me, the song returns to those small choices. I’m interested in care that reaches someone in an ordinary moment, even when we don’t have all the answers.</p>
<p>When someone is already hurting, what could we offer that makes the next moment a little less lonely?</p></div></details>
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-this-is"><summary><span class="mono k">Short song introduction · September 25, 2026</span><b>This is GhostHeart.</b><span class="lead">A heart that survived, and a question about what comes next.</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>This Is GhostHeart begins with a heart that survived and a question about what comes next. The song opens a larger story told through music, film, words and pictures.</p>
<p>What can a person carry forward from survival? And how might that become something another person can recognize themselves in?</p>
<p>The film calls it the name, the scars, and the reason a hidden heart chose to be seen.</p>
<p>In Enter My World, I put it this way: “Maybe my story could help somebody. Even with the ugly parts. Even with the mistakes I have to own.” And then: “If I help one person, then I did something.”</p>
<p>That is the heart of this song. It doesn’t ask anyone to share that past. It asks what a person might carry forward, and who might need to see it.</p>
<p>This Is GhostHeart is one of the eight songs on Before the Heart Wakes. Its Passage, the longer reading for the song, opens Saturday, November 14 at 7 PM Central.</p></div></details>
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-oath"><summary><span class="mono k">Short song introduction · September 25, 2026</span><b>GhostHeart’s Oath</b><span class="lead">What might protecting the light in others look like in an ordinary moment?</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>GhostHeart’s Oath turns from the name to the promise behind it: protecting the light in others.</p>
<p>What might that promise look like in an ordinary moment? What survived can become a reason to help someone else rise.</p>
<p>The mission behind it fits in three words: Build. Serve. Change. Turn survival into work that leaves a door open for someone else.</p>
<p>GhostHeart turns survival into work that serves people who feel unseen. Build the platforms. Tell the truth. Leave a door open behind you.</p>
<p>Sometimes protecting the light might look like noticing who was left out. Sometimes it might mean giving someone the patience you once needed.</p>
<p>GhostHeart’s Oath is one of the eight songs on Before the Heart Wakes. Its Passage, the longer reading for the song, opens Saturday, November 21 at 7 PM Central.</p></div></details>
        <details class="entry panel" id="e-ynG"><summary><span class="mono k">Album spotlight · September 25, 2026</span><b>You’re Not God</b><span class="lead">A question about belief, judgement and the difference between care and control.</span><span class="mono rd">Read the entry ↓</span></summary>
          <div class="entry-body"><p>Where does belief end and control begin?</p>
<p>You’re Not God explores that question through songs about judgement, shame and people who claim the right to speak for heaven. Its focus is what happens when faith is used to hurt or exclude someone.</p>
<p>That Ain’t Holy and Hey Preacher Man ask what happens when compassion becomes control and religion is used to excuse cruelty.</p>
<p>Belief can matter deeply to a person. That does not give someone else the right to decide whose love counts or whose life has value.</p>
<p>This collection is not an attack on faith. It is a confrontation with hypocrisy: purity demanded by people hiding their own sins, scripture used as a weapon, and personal hatred mistaken for God’s voice.</p>
<p>I Won’t Pretend to Speak for You tells it through one long love. One marriage began beneath a church-house light, surrounded by every blessing people could give. Two women began with trembling hands and borrowed flowers. Fifty years later, the song asks what a lifetime of love can tell us that appearances cannot.</p>
<p>That Ain’t Holy is a father-and-son story about poverty, dignity, and who gets judged. Mercy should not check your pockets at the door.</p>
<p>If your faith requires cruelty, that ain’t holy.</p>
<p>The album holds those ideas together: room for faith, and a boundary against cruelty carried out in its name.</p></div></details>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-extra">
          <div class="panel"><b>Passages</b><p>A reading behind each song. New ones on Saturdays at 7 PM Central.</p></div>
          <div class="panel"><b>The long story lives on Substack</b><p>This blog is the short note behind each song. Too Human to Stay Quiet is the long version of GhostHeart’s story, one chapter at a time.</p><a class="btn ghost" href="https://toohumantostayquiet.substack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read on Substack ↗</a></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="alt alt-b" hidden>
        <div class="nb-head">
          <div>
            <p class="mono" style="margin:0 0 6px">New idea · The Notebook</p>
            <h1 class="giant"><span class="chrome">Behind</span> <span class="red">the songs.</span></h1>
            <p style="color:var(--silver);max-width:60ch">The songs don’t tell you everything. Here I write about the thoughts behind them and the questions they leave with me.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="nb-next panel"><span class="mono">Next Passage</span><b class="js-next-sat">Saturday</b><small>7:00 PM Central</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="chips nb-chips"><button type="button" class="chip on" data-nb="all">Everything</button><button type="button" class="chip" data-nb="passage">Passages</button><button type="button" class="chip" data-nb="song">Song notes</button><button type="button" class="chip" data-nb="news">Announcements</button></div>
        <ol class="nb">
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="passage"><time>October 31</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Passage · 3 min read</span><b>Papa Didn’t Raise No Fool — A Backbone and a Heart</b><p class="pull">“The lesson is to keep thinking for yourself without losing your heart.”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>Content note: This reading reflects on family loss, fear, judgment and love, without graphic detail.</p>
<p>Some lessons stay with us because we keep finding places to use them.</p>
<p>In Papa Didn’t Raise No Fool, the narrator remembers hours on a front porch, listening to Papa’s stories. There is affection in that picture, but the stories carry difficult things too: fear, loss and a world that can be harder to understand than it first appears.</p>
<p>Papa takes the questions seriously. He can be stern. He can also send the narrator back to the books with a joke about needing a doctor. The song gives us someone whose care has more than one voice.</p>
<p>At the center of what he teaches are two things that belong together.</p>
<p>Papa didn’t raise no fool He raised me with a backbone and a heart</p>
<p>A backbone gives a person something to stand on when it would be easier to agree for the sake of being accepted. A heart keeps that strength connected to the people affected by what we say and do.</p>
<p>Holding both can take work. We can become so concerned with proving we cannot be fooled that we stop being willing to learn. Or we can become so eager to keep everyone comfortable that we leave our own judgment unused.</p>
<p>Papa’s lesson asks for attention.</p>
<p>Look closely at what is being offered. Take time with the words. A confident voice, an impressive appearance or a familiar place on a screen cannot do our thinking for us. We still have to consider what a claim rests on and remain willing to ask another question.</p>
<p>There is humility in that responsibility. Thinking for yourself includes recognizing when you do not know enough. It includes learning something that changes your mind. Being difficult to fool does not require pretending you have every answer.</p>
<p>The song makes that especially clear when it returns to learning.</p>
<p>The smartest man I ever knew Never finished school</p>
<p>That is the narrator’s regard for one person, not a reason to give up on education. Papa points toward the books himself. The lesson makes room for knowledge gained through study and for wisdom carried in an ordinary life. A person’s title cannot tell us everything worth hearing from them.</p>
<p>The same care belongs in how we judge people.</p>
<p>When Papa talks about love, he refuses to let other people’s shame decide its value. His advice leaves room for a heart to be honest. The strength he is teaching has to include that kindness; otherwise, the backbone has lost something essential.</p>
<p>The final chorus brings fear and hate into the lesson too. They can press for a quick answer about who to follow, whom to distrust and what another person deserves. The song asks the narrator to keep hold of a self that those pressures do not get to command.</p>
<p>That choice reaches beyond a declaration. It can show up in checking a claim before passing it on, hearing someone out before assigning them a motive, or standing by a value when the room would prefer easy agreement.</p>
<p>It can also show up in admitting a mistake and taking the trouble to correct it.</p>
<p>The narrator carries Papa’s words forward because they still have somewhere to go. The porch is the beginning of the lesson. Life beyond it is where the lesson has to be practiced.</p>
<p>He didn’t need a fancy title Didn’t need no polished name</p>
<p>What matters is what the teaching asks of the person who receives it: keep learning, look carefully, love honestly and stand for something without letting cruelty become part of the stance.</p>
<p>A backbone and a heart. Both need to come with us into the next conversation, the next difficult choice, and the next chance to decide what kind of person we want to be.</p>
<p class="note">Some parts of the story are hard to hear. You can pause or step away. If you need support now, see Find Support. <a href="#support">Find Support</a></p></div></details></div></li>
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="news"><time>October 30</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Announcement · 1 min read</span><b>Wake the Heart Week</b><p class="pull">“November 9 to 15: one week inside Before the Heart Wakes, built around the songs and the people they are for.”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>From Monday, November 9 to Sunday, November 15, Before the Heart Wakes gets a week of its own: Wake the Heart Week.</p>
<p>Before the Heart Wakes is eight songs that follow one journey, from witnessing cruelty to finding the courage to stand beside someone else. For one week, every place GhostHeart shows up will carry a different piece of that same story. One heart. Never the same post twice.</p>
<p>Here is what is already set. On Saturday, November 14 at 7 PM Central, Passage 06 opens: the reading for This Is GhostHeart. On Sunday, November 15 at 10 AM Central, the week closes with the Sunday Live.</p>
<p>A Friday night listening event and the first letter to the email list are planned for the same week. Their details will be posted here and sent by email once they are confirmed.</p>
<p>You don’t have to take part in everything. Stay with one song. Carry one line. If someone comes to mind while you listen, that might be the person to share it with.</p>
<p>What survived can help someone rise.</p></div></details></div></li>
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="passage"><time>October 30</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Passage · 2 min read</span><b>It’s Not the 1950s</b><p class="pull">“Change came from the people who refused to wait for it.”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>Some lessons arrive as stories told across a kitchen table.</p>
<p>It’s Not the 1950s grows out of one of those. Grandma told it the way people tell things they have watched happen more than once: the government makes a law, the people push back, and years later the government takes the credit for the change the people fought for.</p>
<p>She was not talking about one fight. She was talking about a pattern.</p>
<p>Slavery did not end because the people in power woke up one day and decided it was wrong. People resisted, escaped, organized and refused to stop. Women did not get the vote because it was handed to them. They marched and argued and kept showing up long after they were told to go home. Same-sex couples did not win the right to marry because someone at the top changed their mind first. They lived openly, told their stories and fought in court until the law caught up.</p>
<p>Each time, the history books can make it sound like the change came from above. Grandma wanted me to remember where it really started.</p>
<p>The change was won by people. The credit was taken by power.</p>
<p>That is not a reason to give up on laws. Laws matter. They can protect people or leave them exposed. It is a reason to remember who moves them.</p>
<p>And the story is not finished. Women today are still fighting for the right to make their own choices about their own lives. Saying “it’s not the 1950s” is not the same as saying the work is done. It is a reminder that the calendar moving forward does not guarantee that freedom moves with it.</p>
<p>What Grandma passed on was not anger for its own sake. It was attention. Notice who is doing the fighting. Notice who shows up to take the bow. And when it is your turn, do not wait for permission to stand up for someone’s freedom, including your own.</p>
<p>The people who changed things were ordinary people who refused to stay quiet.</p>
<p>That door is still open to anyone willing to walk through it.</p>
<p class="note">Some parts of the story are hard to hear. You can pause or step away. If you need support now, see Find Support. <a href="#support">Find Support</a></p></div></details></div></li>
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="passage"><time>October 19</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Passage · 3 min read</span><b>I’m Not — More Than One Chapter</b><p class="pull">“A story can be worth telling before the fear is gone.”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>Sometimes the hardest part of telling a story comes before the first word.</p>
<p>You may know what you want to say and still hear all the ways it could be misunderstood. Someone could choose one sentence, one mistake, one difficult chapter, and decide they have heard enough to explain your whole life.</p>
<p>That fear sits at the beginning of I’m Not. Before the narrator has finished speaking, judgment is already anticipated. The wish to be understood has to share the room with the fear of being reduced to a few words.</p>
<p>And still, there is something worth saying.</p>
<p>I just want to tell my story, So maybe someone will do the same.</p>
<p>There is room in that word “maybe.” It leaves the outcome open. A story might reach someone. It might help them find words for something they have carried quietly. It might give them a reason to consider a next step. The narrator cannot decide what another person will take from it.</p>
<p>What the narrator can choose is whether to speak honestly.</p>
<p>That does not require a perfect ending. There can still be mistakes, uncertainty and parts that are hard to explain. A person can be learning how to live with their choices and still have something worth sharing. If every story had to wait until its teller had nothing left to work on, a great many voices would never be heard.</p>
<p>The song makes room for a person who kept trying.</p>
<p>That matters when a single chapter has become the only one anyone wants to discuss. What went wrong can take up so much space that the effort afterward becomes difficult to see: continuing, trying again, choosing what to do next. Those parts belong to the story too.</p>
<p>No one else can fit a whole person into the worst thing they hear about them. Listening asks more of us than that. It asks us to leave room for what we have not heard, to notice when we are filling in the gaps ourselves, and to let someone finish before we decide what their words must mean.</p>
<p>For the person speaking, honesty can have boundaries.</p>
<p>I’m just opening up the door To the parts I used to hide.</p>
<p>An open door does not mean every room has to be shown. You can choose the part you are ready to put into words. You can keep names, details and other people’s lives private. You can stop where you need to stop. The value of what you share does not depend on giving an audience everything.</p>
<p>And listening to someone else’s story places no obligation on you to reveal your own.</p>
<p>You can recognize a feeling without having lived the same life. You can sit with a line, think about it privately, or leave something unspoken. There is no test of how much you must disclose before the song is allowed to mean something to you.</p>
<p>The bridge holds on to that freedom. Hearing that someone kept going cannot undo all the hurt. The song leaves that truth intact. Its hope is smaller and more possible: perhaps there is still a next step you can choose.</p>
<p>Maybe that step begins with finding words. Maybe it begins with giving yourself time. It belongs to you, and no one else can decide its shape from a distance.</p>
<p>I’m Not ends with an invitation that leaves room for someday.</p>
<p>A story can be offered without applause. It can remain imperfect and still be honest. Speaking can feel frightening and still be a choice worth considering.</p>
<p>For anyone listening, the responsibility is simple enough to begin now: hear the person, leave room for the chapters you do not know, and resist turning one part of a life into the whole of it.</p>
<p>For anyone considering their own words, there is time. You can choose what belongs in the telling and what stays yours.</p>
<p>The fear does not have to disappear before a voice can matter.</p>
<p class="note">Some parts of the story are hard to hear. You can pause or step away. If you need support now, see Find Support. <a href="#support">Find Support</a></p></div></details></div></li>
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="song"><time>October 10</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Song note · 1 min read</span><b>Cruel — Something Needs to Change</b><p class="pull">“What happens after the video ends?”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>That’s the question I want Cruel to leave with us. The crowd moves on, but the person we watched still has to live with what happened.</p>
<p>It’s easy to see one reaction and think we understand someone. What might we be missing? What has made it hard for that person to trust kindness?</p>
<p>One conversation won’t undo years of hurt. Sometimes someone needs more help than we can offer. But a patient response can still matter: time to finish a sentence, a question asked without judgement, or kindness that doesn’t depend on hearing their whole story.</p>
<p>If you’re the person who is hurting, you don’t owe anyone your whole story to deserve kindness.</p>
<p>For me, the song returns to those small choices. I’m interested in care that reaches someone in an ordinary moment, even when we don’t have all the answers.</p>
<p>When someone is already hurting, what could we offer that makes the next moment a little less lonely?</p></div></details></div></li>
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="song"><time>September 25</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Song note · 1 min read</span><b>This is GhostHeart.</b><p class="pull">“A heart that survived, and a question about what comes next.”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>This Is GhostHeart begins with a heart that survived and a question about what comes next. The song opens a larger story told through music, film, words and pictures.</p>
<p>What can a person carry forward from survival? And how might that become something another person can recognize themselves in?</p>
<p>The film calls it the name, the scars, and the reason a hidden heart chose to be seen.</p>
<p>In Enter My World, I put it this way: “Maybe my story could help somebody. Even with the ugly parts. Even with the mistakes I have to own.” And then: “If I help one person, then I did something.”</p>
<p>That is the heart of this song. It doesn’t ask anyone to share that past. It asks what a person might carry forward, and who might need to see it.</p>
<p>This Is GhostHeart is one of the eight songs on Before the Heart Wakes. Its Passage, the longer reading for the song, opens Saturday, November 14 at 7 PM Central.</p></div></details></div></li>
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="song"><time>September 25</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Song note · 1 min read</span><b>GhostHeart’s Oath</b><p class="pull">“What might protecting the light in others look like in an ordinary moment?”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>GhostHeart’s Oath turns from the name to the promise behind it: protecting the light in others.</p>
<p>What might that promise look like in an ordinary moment? What survived can become a reason to help someone else rise.</p>
<p>The mission behind it fits in three words: Build. Serve. Change. Turn survival into work that leaves a door open for someone else.</p>
<p>GhostHeart turns survival into work that serves people who feel unseen. Build the platforms. Tell the truth. Leave a door open behind you.</p>
<p>Sometimes protecting the light might look like noticing who was left out. Sometimes it might mean giving someone the patience you once needed.</p>
<p>GhostHeart’s Oath is one of the eight songs on Before the Heart Wakes. Its Passage, the longer reading for the song, opens Saturday, November 21 at 7 PM Central.</p></div></details></div></li>
          <li class="nb-item" data-typ="song"><time>September 25</time><div class="nb-card"><span class="mono k">Song note · 1 min read</span><b>You’re Not God</b><p class="pull">“A question about belief, judgement and the difference between care and control.”</p><details><summary>Read it</summary><div class="entry-body"><p>Where does belief end and control begin?</p>
<p>You’re Not God explores that question through songs about judgement, shame and people who claim the right to speak for heaven. Its focus is what happens when faith is used to hurt or exclude someone.</p>
<p>That Ain’t Holy and Hey Preacher Man ask what happens when compassion becomes control and religion is used to excuse cruelty.</p>
<p>Belief can matter deeply to a person. That does not give someone else the right to decide whose love counts or whose life has value.</p>
<p>This collection is not an attack on faith. It is a confrontation with hypocrisy: purity demanded by people hiding their own sins, scripture used as a weapon, and personal hatred mistaken for God’s voice.</p>
<p>I Won’t Pretend to Speak for You tells it through one long love. One marriage began beneath a church-house light, surrounded by every blessing people could give. Two women began with trembling hands and borrowed flowers. Fifty years later, the song asks what a lifetime of love can tell us that appearances cannot.</p>
<p>That Ain’t Holy is a father-and-son story about poverty, dignity, and who gets judged. Mercy should not check your pockets at the door.</p>
<p>If your faith requires cruelty, that ain’t holy.</p>
<p>The album holds those ideas together: room for faith, and a boundary against cruelty carried out in its name.</p></div></details></div></li>
        </ol>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="https://toohumantostayquiet.substack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="margin-top:14px">The long story on Substack ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

